window.docContents[159].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Nonlinear-First-Order-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Nonlinear-First-Order-Equations--Hamilton___Jacobi-and-Hopf___Lax--The-equation-and-its-characteristics":{"contents":"The formal Hamilton–Jacobi equation has a Hamiltonian that may depend on\ntime, and the characteristic theory specializes the general first-order characteristic system of the\nprevious chapter to this particular nonlinearity.\n\nFor a Hamiltonian  on\n and a spacetime domain\n, with time in coordinate zero, the Hamilton–Jacobi\nequation is the single-equation problemwith no side conditions;  is the spatial gradient. In the fully\nnonlinear form  the same equation useswhere  is the time component of the spacetime gradient and  its\nspatial part; the value argument  does not enter. The autonomous\nequation  is the special case of a time-independent\nHamiltonian.\n\nLet  be open, let the phase function\n be  in all of its arguments, and let\n satisfy  on , that is,\n. Let  be\ndifferentiable at  with  andand assume that  is differentiable in its momentum argument and jointly\ndifferentiable in its time and space arguments at the point determined by\n. Write , let  be\nthe spacetime gradient along the curve, and let . Then at\nthe parameter value These are Hamilton's equations for  with the curve parameter\nidentified with time; the last identity is the classical\n.\n\nApply the general characteristic identities for  to this\n. Since  is affine in  with unit coefficient, the momentum\ngradient  has time component  and spatial part , which\ngives  and . The general equation\n has , and the spacetime gradient of\n splits into the time derivative  and the spatial gradient\n. Finally , and the equation\n along the solution turns this into .\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Nonlinear First-Order Equations\u0009Hamilton–Jacobi and Hopf–Lax","header":"7.4.2.1. The equation and its characteristics","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Nonlinear-First-Order-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Nonlinear-First-Order-Equations--Hamilton___Jacobi-and-Hopf___Lax--The-equation-and-its-characteristics"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection--Propagation-and-characteristics--Characteristic-geometry":{"contents":"A first-order PDE  collapses to an ODE along curves\ncalled characteristics, determined by the coefficient field . You solve\nthose ODEs and invert the characteristic map back to physical coordinates, and\nyou have a local solution wherever the construction is regular and the map\nremains invertible. Loss of invertibility is one way this local construction\ncan break down.\n\nFor a one-dimensional quasilinear system the characteristic speeds are the\neigenvalues of the coefficient or flux-Jacobian matrix. Hyperbolicity means\nthose speeds are real and the eigenbasis is complete; strict hyperbolicity\nmeans they are also distinct. Each eigenpair gives a characteristic field.\nWhether a matching Riemann invariant exists is a further question, requiring\nintegrability conditions, and the p-system section later constructs them for\nthat particular model.\n\nThe geometry takes a different form for each equation.\n\n* Transport equation (): the solution is\nconstant along characteristics, so  (Evans, 2010,\npp. 18–19).* Wave equation (): the two families of\ncharacteristics  give d'Alembert's formula\n(Evans, 2010, pp. 65–69).* Conservation laws (): characteristics travel at speed\n, which depends on the solution itself. Compressive initial data can\ndrive them together and cause gradient blow-up in finite time. Classical\nregularity then fails; an entropy continuation may contain shocks. This\ngeometric breakdown motivates the move from classical to weak to entropy\nsolutions.* Hamilton–Jacobi (): the characteristics of the\nassociated scalar conservation law determine the gradient in one space\ndimension while the solution is smooth; recovering the solution by spatial\nintegration also requires fixing its time-dependent additive constant\n(Evans, 2010, p. 144; Crandall and Lions,\n1983).\n\nFor comparison, the heat equation\n has a parabolic principal symbol, not a real characteristic cone,\nso nothing limits how fast it propagates. Its Gaussian kernel is positive\neverywhere at every positive time, so nonnegative, nonzero continuous\ncompactly supported data give a positive solution everywhere immediately.\nSign-changing data can cancel at individual points. Hyperbolic equations have\nfinite domains of dependence, set by their characteristic velocities.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Propagation, shocks, and weak selection\u0009Propagation and characteristics","header":"7.1.1.1. Characteristic geometry","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection--Propagation-and-characteristics--Characteristic-geometry"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Finite-Time-Singularity-Interfaces--Riccati-comparison-model":{"contents":"For  with initial value , the explicit candidate isThe formula is total as a Lean function, including at its pole. Analytic\nstatements therefore work on  under .Evans notes the scalar model  before beginning the weighted\neigenfunction argument. The theorem carries a coefficient , an arbitrary\ninitial time, and the horizon formula.\n\nThe formula is only a candidate until its derivative has been computed. The\nrelevant hypothesis is the sign of the product , not separate signs of\n and .\n\nIf  and , the explicit formula satisfies  at\n.\n\nThe denominator factors as\n. Positivity of  and the inequality\n make both factors nonzero. The quotient rule can therefore be applied\nto the explicit formula, and a ring calculation reduces the derivative to\n.\n\nSolving the equation before the horizon is half of the comparison model; the\nother half identifies the limit at the horizon. For divergence to\n, rather than , we assume  and .\n\nIf  and , then  tends to  as  approaches\n from below. The corollary states this as\nNormBlowsUpAt (Riccati.solution a y₀ t₀) (Riccati.horizon a y₀ t₀).\n\nRewrite the solution as . As , the positive\nquantity  tends to zero from the right, so its reciprocal tends to\n; multiplication by  preserves that limit. Positivity of\nthe trajectory identifies order divergence with norm divergence.\n\nWhenever , the solution has the reciprocal-distance formThis algebraic identity is total in ; its analytic use is on the\npre-blow-up interval.\n\nSubstitute the definition of  and factor the original denominator as\n. Since , also ; cancelling this factor\nfrom the numerator and denominator yields . Lean's total\ndivision makes the resulting identity valid even at .\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Finite-Time Behavior\u0009Finite-Time Growth, Lifespan, and Similarity\u0009Finite-Time Singularity Interfaces","header":"6.7.1.5.4. Riccati comparison model","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Finite-Time-Singularity-Interfaces--Riccati-comparison-model"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions","header":"9.2.1. Explicit Representations and Transform Methods","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--B-Splines-and-Schoenberg___Whitney-Collocation--Schoenberg___Whitney-collocation":{"contents":"The Schoenberg–Whitney inequalities place each point  strictly inside\nthe canonical support of , with the points increasing strictly. The\npositivity theorem then gives a positive diagonal, although that alone does\nnot establish nonsingularity.\n\nA family of sample points  satisfies the\nSchoenberg–Whitney condition for the degree- B-splines\n when it is strictly increasing andEach sample point then lies in the open interior of the canonical support of\nits own B-spline, so every diagonal entry  of the collocation\nmatrix is strictly positive, including at repeated interior knots.\n\nThe interlacing inequalities are sufficient for a positive determinant. They\nare not necessary under the half-open endpoint convention: a left-clamped\nB-spline can take value one at its left endpoint.\n\nIf the points  satisfy the Schoenberg–Whitney condition\nfor an arbitrary nondecreasing knot sequence, repeated knots included, thenIn particular the collocation matrix is nonsingular and the B-splines\n are linearly independent real functions.\n\nInsert  copies of each sample point so that its tracked refined\nsupport begins with  equal knots and ends strictly to their right.\nBoehm's knot-insertion formula expresses every\ncoarse B-spline as a nonnegative combination of two adjacent refined\nB-splines, so each insertion factors the coarse collocation matrix as the\nrefined collocation matrix times a bidiagonal matrix with nonnegative\nentries, and such matrices are totally nonnegative on ordered minors. At a\nfully clamped knot the refined B-splines evaluate as a Kronecker delta, so\nafter all insertions the refined collocation rows selected by the sample\npoints form an identity minor. The rectangular Cauchy–Binet formula writes\nthe coarse determinant as a sum of products of ordered minors, all\nnonnegative; tracking one strictly positive ordered minor through every\ninsertion, which the interlacing inequalities guarantee, makes the sum\nstrictly positive. Nonsingularity and the collocation criterion then give\nlinear independence.\n\nThe geometric theorem certifies collocation for B-splines at interlaced\npoints. A numerical backend often holds a concrete matrix and a computed\nfactorization instead, and wants nonsingularity without invoking any theory\nof the basis; for that purpose the factorization itself, with unit-triangular\nfactors and positive pivots, is the certificate, since its determinant is\nthe product of the pivots.\n\nFor a square matrix  over a linearly ordered commutative ring with\nstrictly ordered multiplication, a positive  certificate consists of\nmatrices ,  and pivots  withwhere  is lower triangular with unit diagonal and  is upper\ntriangular with unit diagonal. The certificate stores no determinant\nassertion; a validated backend supplies the factors, and Lean checks the\nproduct, the triangular structure, the unit diagonals, and the positivity of\nevery pivot.\n\nChecking a certificate is exact arithmetic: multiply the three factors,\ncompare with , and inspect the diagonals and the signs of the pivots. The\nfamily behind  need not consist of B-splines.\n\nA valid positive  certificate for  proveshence . When  is the collocation matrix of a finite family\nof functions at matching points over a linearly ordered field, the family is\ntherefore linearly independent.\n\nThe determinant is multiplicative, the determinant of a triangular matrix is\nthe product of its diagonal entries, which are all one, and the determinant\nof the diagonal factor is the product of the pivots, a product of strictly\npositive elements. The collocation criterion converts nonvanishing of the\ndeterminant into linear independence.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods\u0009B-Splines and Schoenberg–Whitney Collocation","header":"9.2.1.10.2. Schoenberg–Whitney collocation","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--B-Splines-and-Schoenberg___Whitney-Collocation--Schoenberg___Whitney-collocation"}});